import "core"
import hsrawprgm()


module(Json)
  # # Reading JSON
    An importer: a way of reading files written in another syntax, declared in Catln rather than
    built into the compiler. A file which writes `import "json/importer.ct"` on one line may write
    `import json("config.json")` on the next, and the compiler will read that file by building this
    page and running what is declared below. A file whose name ends in `.json` which nothing
    imported by name is read with this too, found through @importers.ct@.
    What a JSON file is read AS is a value and not a program, which is what makes this the second
    kind of importer rather than a second instance of the first. @python/importer.ct@ reads a file
    for the declarations it makes, and its page holds a function per function. This reads a file for
    the one thing it is, and its page holds one declaration whose body is the whole document as a
    [/Data/Json]. Both go through the one route, which is what says the route carries more than
    the shape it was written for.
    The reading itself is behind [#runtime], as Python's is. Reading JSON is a solved problem with a
    library already among the compiler's dependencies, and rewriting it in Catln for the sake of
    saying it was written in Catln would be a worse reader and no more of an importer. What has
    moved is where the importer is DECLARED, from a table in the compiler to a page a program can
    import.
    This page writes `import hsrawprgm()`, because that is where [/Catln/ImportResult] and the raw
    program inside it are declared. No page of @stack/core/@ may write that import — see
    @core/hsprgm.ct@, which says why — and this page is not one of them.
  parseJson(path -> String) -> /Catln/ImportResult..
    # [parseJson] reads the JSON file at [path] as a page holding one declaration: the whole of the
      file as a [/Data/Json], under a name built from the path. The name is built rather than fixed
      because a program importing two JSON files is an ordinary thing to do, and a fixed name would
      make the second of them silently the first. It is written into the page as a heading, since a
      name nobody would guess is the one part of the page a reader has to reproduce by hand.
      The page imports @json/json.ct@, which declares the type its value is built out of.
      An object's members arrive in the order the reader hands them over, which is alphabetical by
      key rather than the order they were written in — that is what the JSON library the reading
      rests on does with an object, and a document whose keys are shown sorted is the cost of it.
      Two members written under one key are one member for the same reason.
    #importer(name= "json", extensions= "json")
    #runtime("jsonImport")
